00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.350
So let's take a look at
an overview of the trends

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:27.910
for large language models.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.590
So one of the aspects
that has been mind blowing

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:36.710
since GPT 3 came out
is that as you scale up

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:40.410
the number of parameters in
a model, they get better.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:45.230
And what this means is that
language models like Bert and T5

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:45.850
were good.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.690
But as we increase the
number of parameters,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:48.730
they got really better.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.350
They perform much better.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.470
And what this led to is
something called a scaling

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.590
loss for large
language models, which

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.720
are used in building
the base model for--

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.360
the pre-trained base model.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.848
And as you increase
the amount of compute--

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.140
so there are three graphs here.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.840
As you increase the amount
of compute on the x-axis,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.860
the loss function on
the y-axis goes down.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.400
And the more compute
you have put in,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.980
the more the test
loss goes down,

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.480
which leads to a better model.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.640
So that was one big
aspect that led to GPT 3,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.640
then all the subsequent--

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.040
ChatGPT, PaLM,
Gemini, and so on.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.520
And then similarly,
as you increase

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.040
the data set size on
x-axis, the test loss

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.380
goes down further on the y-axis.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.480
So that's another aspect of--

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.320
another axis of scaling that
leads to better language models.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.180
And the third one is, of
course parameter counts.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.080
So if you increase the number
of layers in a transformer

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.500
or the amount of parameters that
are there in the language model,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.200
that improves the
loss value, and that

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.380
leads to a better model.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.780
And this has been the
foundation for a lot of-- this

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.840
has been the foundation
for a long time,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.940
until last year, where
this was starting

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.140
to hit some kind of
a saturation point.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.740
So from 2018 to almost 2024--

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.680
this graph is a
little bit outdated.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.220
But from 2018 to 2024,
as the model size

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.260
has gone up almost
consistently, we

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.320
have been increasing
the size of the model.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.260
So you started with
something like Bert

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.240
was 340 million parameters, GPT
2 was 1.5 billion parameters,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.400
and then GPT 3 was 175
billion parameters,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.520
and then PaLM was 540 billion.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.360
And then there was a bunch
of subsequent models.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.300
And GPT 4 is estimated
to be there, which

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.840
is trillions of parameters.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.340
So you can imagine
that there has

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.380
been an exponential
growth in just the model

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.900
size for large language models.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.560
The large stands for that
they're growing in size.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.100
And that has been one of the key
areas in which the models have

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.470
been getting better.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.850
So why do we care about
the models getting bigger?

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.090
What does this lead to?

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:01.550
So as I was explaining that
as the models get bigger,

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:05.150
they continuously improve in
performance on natural language

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.270
benchmarks on various
other benchmarks, reasoning

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:08.950
benchmarks, and so on.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:10.710
And then the other
interesting aspect

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.230
that I will show
you in a moment is

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.710
that they learn how to
do few shot learning.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.350
So earlier, you
have to fine tune

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.930
the model for specific domain.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.250
But just by giving
a few examples,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.310
the model can follow
that template and reason

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.670
in that same vein,
which makes it extremely

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.170
easy to prototype things.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.922
And then the third aspect
is that as the models become

00:05:32.922 --> 00:05:34.630
bigger, they have
emergent behavior where

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:39.750
the capabilities like reasoning
only emerge in larger models.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.670
So just to understand few shot
learning or zero shot learning

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:43.390
here.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:48.230
So if the model is given
a question or a task,

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.810
like translate English to
French, it's able to give--

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:52.940
so this is called a prompt.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.190
Like it's a given a task,
translate English to French.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.890
And then it's asked
to translate cheese

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.550
to whatever is the French word.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:02.250
If the model can predict the
answer without actually being

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.930
trained on the specific
task, then that

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.490
would be called
zero shot learning.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.170
And then few shot
learning, in addition to

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.550
giving the description
of the task,

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.183
you're also giving
a few examples.

00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:13.850
So in this particular
case, it's giving,

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.150
translate English to French.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.850
And it's given a few
examples of the translation

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.030
from English to French.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:20.850
And then it's asked
to translate cheese

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.350
and it's able to translate that.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.050
And you can see that
this capability shows up

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.710
in the large language models
across a large number of tasks.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.490
And that has been
one of the key areas

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.990
that GPT 3, PaLM, and
other models enabled.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.490
And then that
subsequent innovations

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:39.410
came from reasoning
aspects of this.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:43.713
So on the reasoning side, as
models have become bigger,

00:06:43.713 --> 00:06:45.630
they have this emergent
behavior of reasoning,

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.820
which Azalia will tell us
a little bit more about.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.340
So as the models
become bigger, not only

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:54.980
we have this
predictive scaling laws

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:58.060
property that we know how
the loss function is going

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.020
to go down as we throw
in more compute data

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.060
and parameters in
the model, there

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:07.540
are new behaviors that
appear in the model

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.000
that they didn't have before.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.140
And we never could predict that
until we had these bigger models

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.900
and saw this behavior in them.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.620
And one of the most important--

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.580
one of those behaviors is the
chain of thought behavior.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.300
And for those of you who
are not familiar with this,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:28.580
here is how it works.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.040
So in a usual
prompting of a model,

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:36.940
we can ask a model, in
this case, a math problem.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:39.320
And then we can give
it a one shot example.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.680
Here is a math problem,
here is the answer.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:47.500
Now I ask you a similar but not
exactly the same math problem.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:51.240
And the model is
expected to answer that.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.380
And the model might be able
to use the one shot example,

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.820
or it might not be.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:08:00.680
But we can do better here
by not only give an example,

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.720
but also provide the reasoning
or the chain of thought

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.760
on how we got to that answer.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:10.620
So in this case, the question
is a very simple question.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.580
Roger has five tennis balls.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:16.440
He buys two more
cans of tennis balls.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.340
Each can has three tennis balls.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.840
How many total tennis
balls does he have now?

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.960
And instead of just
saying 11, you're

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.080
walking the model through
how to get to this.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.740
Roger started with five balls.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.400
Two cans of three tennis balls
is 6, and 5 plus 6 is 11.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.440
And with this example,
then the model

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.900
has become familiar with the
process to get to the answer.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.450
And it can leverage that
to answer new problems.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.150
Well, obviously, this is
a very simple problem.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.550
Any 1B parameter model
nowadays can solve this.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:56.910
Doesn't need an example or a
few shot or a chain of thought.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.530
But the chain of
thought property

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:01.910
itself is a property that
is holding to this day.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.730
And it's a very, very
important property

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.050
for the reasoning models
and all the thinking models

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:12.930
and much of the progress that
we have seen in the past year

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:14.770
or so.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:18.770
Now, this property
again appears as we

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.590
have larger and larger models.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:24.090
Here are three models,
LaMDA, GPT, and PaLM.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.150
And as you can see,
the chain of thought.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:30.450
And this is some results
on some math data set.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.130
And these models,
when they're smaller,

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:38.770
like eight billion parameter
for LaMDA, or for GPT,

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.970
around 7 billion,
they can't really

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.990
benefit from chain of thought.

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:44.690
It doesn't do anything for them.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.750
But as they become larger
or for a pull mother

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:52.670
again at 8 billion, it can
leverage this chain of thought

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:57.070
property and learn from the
reasoning and the process that

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:03.630
is provided in the context in
order to solve problems better.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.350
And not only that, there
are other abilities

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:10.130
to solve new tasks as we
increase the size of the models.

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:13.190
Like, for examples,
models can all of a sudden

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:19.310
solve mod arithmetics better or
solve word unscrambled problem

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:23.330
just by having this larger
size and this property.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:27.430
You see that all of a sudden,
it appears at a certain size.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:30.310
And because of all
of this, we still,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.810
as in frontier
companies and labs,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.790
are still interested in
pushing the scaling laws

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:40.100
and not only get the
natural kind of progression,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.680
but also we will
see more and more

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:49.520
of these emergent behavior that
is going to be very important.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.380
Now, a little bit
of history here.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:58.040
So ChatGPT was launched
in November 2022.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:03.920
Obviously, it was one of the
most successful apps or software

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.360
ever created.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.040
The time to reach one
million users for it

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:15.440
was five days, which is
significantly faster than many

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.780
of the other famous software
that you can see there,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.500
or services that
you can see there.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.640
And there were
pieces of innovation

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.760
on top of just scaling up the
parameter size of the model that

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:32.440
were very important to
make ChatGPT what it was

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:37.360
and to make that leapfrog
over the previous models,

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.340
like GPT-3, so on.

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:45.340
And two important pieces here
were the instruction tooling

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:50.340
and the reinforcement learning
from human feedback, which

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.683
we are going to briefly
mention in the next slides.

00:11:52.683 --> 00:11:54.100
But throughout
this course, we are

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.440
going to learn more
and more about them.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.900
And then we're going
to learn about all

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.240
of these in more details.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:09.600
But let's just take a step back
and see how the process works.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.840
So the pre-training
step is the step

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:17.620
at the beginning of any
language model training.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.280
That's the easiest step.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.420
And we just train the model
to predict the next token out

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.460
of all sorts of text
and data, and so on.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.800
And then this is
the fine tuning step

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:33.540
that was a key differentiating
factor for a model like ChatGPT.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:38.150
And let's start with
the first aspect

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:41.150
of fine tuning,
which is alignment

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.470
with human preferences.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:47.050
So when a model is
pre-trained, it has no sense.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:50.610
It has seen all data on
the internet, in the books,

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:51.370
everywhere.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:55.510
But it has no sense of what is
right and wrong and what is.

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:58.970
It just statistically knows
about the state of the worl,

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.550
but does it doesn't
exactly know things or know

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:03.230
how to follow instructions.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:07.030
So the next step in
training these models

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:11.150
is the step where we
try to steer the AI

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:16.310
models to follow the goals
and preferences and values

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.830
of humans.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.810
And this is still a big problem.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:21.950
It's a big issue.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.330
We haven't mastered it yet.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:28.070
But as we progress,
models are becoming

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:35.570
better and much more powerful
in the things that they can do.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:39.150
So it turns out that we can fine
tune the models to be aligned.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:42.690
For example, here are
some graphs that show,

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:47.290
what if we take our base model
that's just pre-trained and fine

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:51.370
tune it for things like
sensibleness or safety?

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:56.150
And these are data
that we have collected.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:59.850
These are high quality
data for showing the model

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:04.210
what we mean by what's safe and
what's unsafe or what's sensible

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:05.790
and what doesn't make sense.

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.450
And these are more curated,
more highly curated data

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.130
that we pass to the
model for fine tuning

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:13.730
and to make the
model more aligned

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:17.870
with the values or the
preferences of humans.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:26.890
So going back, let's just walk
through this process again.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:32.540
So the first stop when we take
a pre-trained language model

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:36.700
is fine tuning with next
token prediction, which

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.560
follows the same
process as pre-training.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.500
But this time on data that
is much more higher quality.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:50.260
For example, this could be data
from books, creative essays.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.420
Data that companies
pay a whole lot,

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.540
millions of dollars or
hundreds of millions

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.340
of dollars potentially,
to buy this data that's

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:58.720
really high quality.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:14:59.840
And then we train them.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.460
We fine tune the model
on those, and the model

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.120
becomes much better
as a result of that.

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:11.380
The next step is
instruction tuning.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.740
And by instruction
tuning, what we mean

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.300
is that we show the model.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:19.280
Again, traditionally
in the past two years,

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:22.740
this instruction tuning
data is a combination

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.340
of human generated
data role templates

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:28.160
and the synthetic data.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.840
But the way this instruction
fine tuning works

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:37.760
is that we do have instruction
and then question answer pairs,

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.000
where the model learns how
to follow the questions

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:41.940
and answers them.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:46.080
For example, here, an
instruction tuning data set

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.400
could be about the weather.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.140
Please answer the
following question,

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:52.940
what is the boiling
point of nitrogen?

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:55.040
And then this is the answer.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.240
Or we can have chain of
thought fine tuning, where

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:02.200
we show the model how it
can walk through a process

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:03.420
to get to the answer.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:05.300
And then we have the label.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:07.300
We have the result over here.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.360
And this is like these pairs
of questions and instructions

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:11.580
and answers.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:16.720
And again, this
data set is very--

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:19.060
there's a lot of effort that
goes into this data set.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:22.800
And the quality of data set
and the generality of it

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.600
has a lot of impact on
the quality of the model

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.770
that we get at this stage.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.770
And after this
point, then the model

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:35.770
becomes more and more like
the models we see today.

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:39.670
You can ask them questions, you
can go back and forth with them.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:44.010
They have this sense of
how to perceive a question

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:51.130
and how to walk through some
process to get to an answer.

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:54.610
And the next step after
instruction fine tuning

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:59.930
is this process of using
human preferences and fine

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:01.910
tuning the model based on that.

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:05.050
And this process is called
RLHF, or Reinforcement

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:08.089
Learning from Human Feedback.

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:10.490
The way this is different
from the previous step

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:13.170
is the way we create
the objective function

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:15.109
and create the data.

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:20.089
So in this case, instead of just
creating some supervised prompt

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:24.369
and labels, we are basically
creating a reward model

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:27.119
out of human preferences.

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:33.380
So companies spend a lot of
money asking humans, sometimes

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:38.900
experts, sometimes just humans,
normal humans, about questions

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:41.700
and the answers
generated by the model.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.620
And we asked the
humans to rate them,

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:47.480
to see which one is correct,
and which one is not correct,

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.900
and that's how we can create
some sort of a reward model

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.340
here.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:55.560
And this reward model
is instead of human.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:58.140
Then we can use the
reward model in order

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:01.500
to guide the
parameters of our LLM

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.460
towards generating
answers that are

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.160
in line with this reward model.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:09.060
So basically we
want the generations

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:12.720
by the model to be such that
this reward model says yes,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.700
these are good generations.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.600
We're going to learn
more about this here,

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:20.300
but we wanted you to
have the high level

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:24.580
idea of what RLHF means.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:26.380
We can have different
reward types,

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:27.780
different types of reward.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:29.680
For example, we
could ask the model

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:31.800
to reward a pair
of answers based

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.960
on their correctness or
helpfulness or specificity

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.120
or harmlessness.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.900
All reward functions
that we can define.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:41.720
And depending on how we
want the models to be

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:45.640
and what we care most about, we
can weigh these different reward

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:50.400
models and use that
in the RLHF process.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:54.020
So these step at a
high level, of course,

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:55.780
there's a lot of
effort goes into that.

00:18:55.780 --> 00:19:01.680
But pre-training and then fine
tuning on higher quality data

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:06.800
instruction tuning and RLHF
were the core components

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:12.640
that made up a model like
ChatGPT over something that

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:17.080
existed, over all the
large models like GPT-3

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:18.540
and prior models.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.720
So that made a huge difference
in how good and capable

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:22.950
the models were.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:29.570
So that was very cool
up until last year.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.190
Or a year and a half ago.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:35.530
Pre-training and then fine
tuning were the big pieces.

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:38.130
But since a year
and a half ago, it

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:41.970
turns out inference is
also a frontier for making

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:45.090
the models much more capable.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:50.290
So and that opened a
whole set of whole lot

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.330
of new research and
directions and ways

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.330
that we can make
the model better.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:59.050
So let's talk about
it a little bit.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:03.770
Like this is the work we
did in my lab last year.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:08.250
Last summer, and it was
called Large Language Monkeys.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:11.270
And it's inspired by the
infinite monkey theorem.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:14.730
And it's related to
inference scaling.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.930
How many of you have heard of
the infinite monkey theorem?

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:20.450
OK, that's a good amount.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.710
So the idea of the infinite
monkey theorem, what it says

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.370
is that it's not proven.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:30.830
But what it says is that if
you have a monkey and a typing

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:35.070
machine, and then we keep
letting the monkey type all day

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:37.630
forever, after
some time, we will

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:40.830
have the works of William
Shakespeare in the things

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:43.390
that the monkey types.

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:46.790
So we were inspired by
that to name our project.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:50.430
But the idea here
was that, let's have

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.670
the LLM be the monkey.

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:57.750
And let's ask the model
over and over again

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:01.430
to solve a given input problem.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:05.090
You can think of it as parallel
sampling or parallel generation.

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:08.050
So instead of asking the model
once to solve the input problem,

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:10.170
you can ask it many times.

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:13.230
And also, let's assume you
have some sort of verifier

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:18.040
or selection mechanism so you
can pick which one of these

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:21.680
generated responses
were correct.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:23.840
And then you output
that in your system.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.180
That becomes the final output.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.640
So the model, instead of
generating one answer,

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:30.540
generates many, many answers.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:33.120
And then your verifier
selects which one of them

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.680
is correct and then outputs
one of the correct ones.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.100
You can think of a verifier
as, say, some unit tests.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:42.380
If the problem is
generating code,

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:44.640
the verifier could
be the unit test

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:46.920
that we run against
the code, and we

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.160
see which one of
the code generation

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:53.200
passes all the unit
tests, and so on.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:59.040
The reason this can
be a possibility here

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:03.160
is that models are not--

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.000
there is some
variance in the way

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.180
the models generate responses.

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:08.600
It's not deterministic.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:12.160
And you can even control the
variation in the model responses

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:13.920
with this thing
called temperature.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:16.980
So you can force the model
or encourage the model

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:23.900
to generate more different
responses as you ask a question.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:28.060
And here, now let's see some
of the results of this repeated

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:29.140
sampling.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:34.140
For a bunch of tasks, like
math and coding benchmarks,

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:39.420
we increased the number of
samples per problem from 1,

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:43.100
which is the normal
one, to 10,000.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:46.580
And here, we are showing
the coverage or the fraction

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:50.980
of problems that are solved by
at least one of these samples.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:55.320
And what we are seeing here
is that so the red line,

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:59.380
the red dashed line,
was the GPT-4o model.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:05.000
And while these blue and green
lines, which were the normal HP,

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:10.140
the normal 3HP and 7AB, were
worse than GPT-4o model with one

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:10.640
sample.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:15.090
But if we increase the number
of samples from these models,

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:21.910
in all of these cases, they do
better than the GPT-4o model.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:26.790
And what it says is that it kind
of seems like the models already

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:31.070
know a whole lot more than
what you get out of them when

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:33.190
you just ask them once, right.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:38.290
So this is the underlying
property of inference scaling,

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:43.310
because it seems like we can
get a whole lot more capability

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:46.470
and problem solving
ability from these models

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.710
if we just bring in
this inference scaling.

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.770
Here, we are showing
repeated sampling,

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:54.990
but there are many
other ways to do that.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:56.910
So the reason it's
called inference scaling

00:23:56.910 --> 00:24:00.410
is that we are not touching any
of the parameters of the model.

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:03.770
The model is fixed, and we
are just at inference time.

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:06.430
We are creating different
ways to generate,

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:09.110
to produce generations
from the model

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:12.730
and use them to generate
higher quality answers

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:15.890
or answers to harder
problems for the model.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:19.410
The interesting part here is
that for some of these problems,

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:23.970
out of these 10,000 solutions,
maybe three or four of them were

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:26.030
correct for a single problem.

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:31.850
So it shows that
how important it

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:34.690
is to scale the
inference to really get

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:38.530
to the core capability
of the models.

00:24:38.530 --> 00:24:41.410
But what is the
difference of ways

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:44.210
of traversing all of the
possible answers and just

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:45.130
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:57.750
Sorry, what's the question?

00:24:57.750 --> 00:24:59.850
What is the difference
of just ways

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:04.810
of traversing all the possible
answers and [INAUDIBLE]?

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:07.890
So the possible answers
need to be generated somehow

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.040
by the model, right?

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:15.540
So the space of all possible
answers to the model

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:18.000
is way bigger.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.760
if we do like a tree
search or a random search,

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:22.100
it's way bigger, right.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:23.980
There's no way to hit.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:29.260
Even imagine we are asking a
human, just sit down and solve

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:30.700
this problem, right.

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:36.200
Of course, there's no
limit to human creativity,

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:37.160
human's creativity.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:40.160
But there is a limit to how many
generations we can have, right.

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:42.660
Or if we randomize
things, there's

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:46.120
a whole lot set of answers
that can be generated.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:50.120
And this is highly
sample efficient, right.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:51.880
10,000 is not a whole lot.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:56.620
Especially like, for
example, for the math f to f.

00:25:56.620 --> 00:26:00.660
Some of these problems are
really hard IMO level problems.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:04.620
These are really hard problems
that a tiny model like a 7

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.860
and Llama ATP can solve.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:11.320
I was thinking, how
does the latency compare

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:15.760
to when you do actually generate
all those samples compared

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:18.200
to the GPT-4o, right?

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:20.070
How does the latency--

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.880
is tradeoff for you?

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.320
Yeah, of course,
there's a tradeoff.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:27.640
And we touch on
that in the lecture

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:29.840
that I go more
details towards this.

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:31.840
The parallel samples,
the good thing about them

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:34.720
that they can be
run in parallel.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:37.780
So from a latency perspective,
that's less of an issue.

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:39.720
But of course, there
is the frontier

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:43.320
of cost tradeoffs between how
much compute you're spending.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:44.900
And we will look into that.

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:45.820
And it varies.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:48.440
It varies depending on
the type of and complexity

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:50.400
of the problems.

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:51.600
Yes?

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:55.480
This approach where you have
a verifiable domain like math

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:59.540
and coding and how do you
handle it when you don't have--

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.360
Yes, great question again.

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:04.800
When you have verifiers,
it's much easier.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:06.650
When you don't have
verifiers, there's

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:09.210
this whole set of
research on how

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:14.050
to train LLM as Judge or
LLMs reward functions or LLMs

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:14.983
with tools becoming.

00:27:14.983 --> 00:27:16.650
And we're going to
learn about that all.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:19.330
We have a lecture just
dedicated to verifiers, so.

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:22.570
Yeah?

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:23.970
Just a quick question, sorry.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:29.970
So does this generalize to
any positive temperature?

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:35.530
So as the limit, as the
temperature goes to infinity--

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:37.050
No.

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:39.490
Yeah, we can have the
temperature being too high,

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:41.730
because it's going
to be gibberish.

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:43.630
The model doesn't generate.

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:46.970
So there's actually
a research project

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:51.110
last year was on tuning that or
figuring out what temperature.

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:57.070
Usually if you go beyond 1.2
or so, it's not great, yeah.

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:58.090
Yeah.

00:27:58.090 --> 00:27:59.890
But there are other
tricks that you can

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:06.190
do to make the model be more--

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:09.550
have more diversity in
the type of answers.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.750
And that can be useful.

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:13.370
All right.

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:20.030
So let's look at this.

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:25.150
So now, again, going back to
the events of last year, year

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:28.190
and a half, we had
the DeepSeek model.

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:31.450
I think it came out in December.

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:32.390
Christmas.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:36.110
DeepSeek came out December 2024.

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:37.230
Oh, wow.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:38.910
It's been a short time.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:42.590
Short time or long
time, I don't know.

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:46.210
So then models like
DeepSeek came last year.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:49.550
So basically, a core
innovation in DeepSeek.

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:53.310
And then the one series Gemini
thinking and such were that.

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:57.030
Now we are bringing this fine
tuning and test time scaling

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:58.150
together.

00:28:58.150 --> 00:29:00.990
Because with this test
time scaling and the models

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:04.280
themselves, we have
this new engine

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:09.240
that we can generate a
whole lot of synthetic data.

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:12.440
For example, for problems
like for math problems,

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:13.620
where we know the answer.

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:17.160
We can have the model generate
different answers that leads

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:21.160
to that final golden answer.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.120
Or for the coding problems,
we can use the model

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:28.120
to generate tons of data
during test time scaling, tons

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:31.020
of quality data to
solve coding problems.

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:33.180
And that becomes part
of our training set.

00:29:33.180 --> 00:29:38.060
Now we can use that to fine
tune the model to become better.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.440
So that, bringing
these two together

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:46.080
became a big piece in thinking
models and reasoning models.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:52.380
And this is an area that is
really, really important.

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:54.600
There's just so much
to be explored in this.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:58.040
Because again, it's
open-ended, right.

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:01.780
There's no boundary in how good
the models can become with test

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:04.540
time scaling, and then
bringing that back

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:07.580
to the process of training the
model or fine tuning the model

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:10.280
to become better
is very exciting.

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:12.940
And that's the
self-improving piece

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:17.020
that we are very excited about.

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:20.260
So a little bit about
reasoning models.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:23.600
In the language monkeys, what we
were showing was the coverage.

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:27.340
If we had access
to verifiers, we

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:32.620
can see the log linear scaling
laws with the number of samples.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:38.580
And OpenAI, when they
released o1 last September,

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:41.640
they showed the log
linear relationship,

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:47.640
this time with pass at one for
these hard aiming benchmark,

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:51.680
which is a set of really
difficult math questions.

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:55.460
So what they're showing
here is that as they're

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:57.660
increasing test time
compute, and this

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:03.050
is in log scale, the
accuracy, the past one

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:05.510
accuracy of the model goes up.

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:07.910
And this is for test time alone.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:11.450
Previously, this has
been shown for training,

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:14.150
but it's very interesting
that this kind of scaling

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:18.430
applies to test time as well,
without changing the parameter

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:19.690
count of the model.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:26.790
I'm going to walk you
through a few examples of how

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:33.030
this reasoning or test
time scaling works

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.870
with models like O3 or Gemini.

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:40.230
So one motivation here is that
for difficult problems, just

00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:43.810
like humans, think a lot
more, spend a lot more time,

00:31:43.810 --> 00:31:46.830
or consider many
different strategies,

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:48.750
thinking models do the same.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:52.750
And they may use a chain of
thought or other techniques

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:57.210
to just go about
solving a problem.

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:00.430
And there are different
steps to that.

00:32:00.430 --> 00:32:04.270
For example, problem
analysis is one step.

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:08.250
The model first sees a problem
and tries to analyze it.

00:32:08.250 --> 00:32:11.050
Then it can do the
task decomposition.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:16.810
Breaking a task into simpler
tasks that are more addressable.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:22.450
Then there is the self-evolution
strategies where the model tries

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.470
something, sees the feedback.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:29.930
For example, runs some
tests on the code or uses

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:32.650
a calculator, or just
judges the answer.

00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:36.050
And it can use that
feedback to optimize itself.

00:32:36.050 --> 00:32:39.630
There's self-correction, and
there are alternative proposals.

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:41.170
If something doesn't
work, the model

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:46.050
can backtrack and try
a different approach.

00:32:46.050 --> 00:32:50.930
These are some key principles
that the model probably

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:54.330
at some point, pieces
of it were trained

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:58.600
as part of the training data
set were curated by humans.

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:03.460
But a big part of it is also
the model acquiring these skills

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:07.940
during this fine
tuning process and RL

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:11.980
from synthetic data process
that the model does.

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:17.540
Here is an example of
how o1 does analysis.

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:22.980
The model is asked to write a
bash script that takes a matrix

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:26.520
and then outputs the
transpose of that matrix.

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:29.400
And the model starts
with, just like humans,

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:34.440
starts thinking, what are the
pieces, important pieces, here?

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:37.780
So the user is
requesting a bash script.

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:43.300
Let's understand the input and
output formats of this matrix,

00:33:43.300 --> 00:33:44.240
and so on.

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:47.220
So the model just thinks itself.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:52.640
And amazingly, it's like a
progression of chain of thought.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:55.353
This is like helping the
model finding the answer.

00:33:55.353 --> 00:33:57.520
But the difference between
chain of thought and this

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:02.880
is that the model itself is
producing this chain of thought.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:06.880
There's, of course, task
decomposition, in this case,

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:09.040
for the approach.

00:34:09.040 --> 00:34:11.699
The model thinks about
parsing the input,

00:34:11.699 --> 00:34:17.199
building the matrix as an
array of arrays and so on.

00:34:17.199 --> 00:34:20.760
There's also self-correction,
which is a big important piece

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:22.000
of--

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:23.657
this is an important piece here.

00:34:23.657 --> 00:34:25.199
And I don't know
how many of you have

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:27.600
seen that when trying
with the models

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:30.080
that you can see
the thinking traces.

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:31.800
And this is actually
pretty common.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:35.000
The models start something, and
then in the middle says, wait,

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:36.040
the correct--

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:39.800
there's something wrong, maybe.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:41.780
And maybe I need to fix that.

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:45.719
And it's very, very cool that
the model can do that itself.

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:49.440
So reasoning models, when they
came out, like models like o1,

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:54.489
compared to a model like GPT-4o,
which wasn't a reasoning model,

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:58.470
they tend to be better in
obviously the reasoning task.

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:02.210
For example, in math
calculation, data analysis,

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:06.530
programming and such,
they all outperform

00:35:06.530 --> 00:35:11.050
GPT-4o, but not necessarily
in personal writing or editing

00:35:11.050 --> 00:35:12.890
texts, and so on.

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:16.010
Yeah, question?

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:18.250
Do you think the
gains due to this

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.210
asking for a reasonable
price is due to actually

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:23.450
the act of generating
this reasoning traits,

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:27.170
or just the act of asking
the model to think or do

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:29.030
these decomposition at all?

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.990
Like, for example, if you
said, think step by step,

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:35.770
but don't generate
out loud, but actually

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:39.570
generate false positive
[INAUDIBLE] without saying.

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:41.970
Yeah.

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:43.530
I repeat the question.

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:46.650
You were saying, do you
think the reasoning models

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:49.920
are becoming what they are?

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:53.420
Is it based on the
loud way of thinking?

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:58.020
I think the question is that,
is it because of the reasoning,

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:01.290
or is it because of things step
by step, the chain of thought?

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.040
Is it the data that's
making a difference,

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:05.900
or is it the instruction?

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:10.420
I mean, so the data basically
has made the model become

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:14.820
a generalized thinker, right.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:19.320
And all of these steps,
breaking down a problem,

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:22.600
being able to backtrack,
being able to do analysis,

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:25.340
all of these are these
skills that the model

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:29.260
has learned during this thinking
optimization process that

00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:31.440
now generalize to other things.

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:33.757
And they're helping
the model right now.

00:36:33.757 --> 00:36:35.340
So I think the other
way to look at it

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:37.560
is as Azalia presented,
repeated sampling.

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:41.300
So if you had just a base
model right after pre-training,

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:44.050
it would be able to
do some reasonings.

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:46.300
But at the same time, it
will generate different kinds

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:47.580
of reasoning chains.

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:50.280
And it doesn't quite know
which one is correct.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:53.760
So a lot of what we
will cover in train time

00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:55.680
or test time scaling
really comes down

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:57.740
to it learns, which is correct.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:36:59.500
So pass it, one
accuracy goes up,

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:01.800
which is what you were seeing
in the reasoning models.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:04.640
As opposed to pass
it k or coverage,

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.420
which is what you were seeing in
the repeated sampling results.

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:07.920
Yeah.

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:14.800
And one other way to think about
it, yes, these deep thinking

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:16.880
and things that
the model generates

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:19.960
is actually helping
the model every time

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:22.800
to solve a new problem.

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:26.120
And although it's very
expensive for the model

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:30.320
to generate those things, still,
we want them to generate those,

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:35.840
because it leads
to better answers.

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:37.100
Any other questions?

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:37.840
Yes?

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:40.200
Can you use a different
model for the reasoning step

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.360
versus generating
the final answer?

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.200
Maybe a smaller
or a purpose built

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:49.970
one just in case the final
answer is a large problem?

00:37:49.970 --> 00:37:51.930
So typically, the
reasoning capabilities

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:53.710
have gone up with
the model size.

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:58.830
So if anything, you would use
the larger reasoning traces.

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:01.690
And then maybe you collect
a bunch of reasoning traces

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:04.490
and then have a smaller
model summarize the answer.

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:08.650
So the models, this is at least
currently, things may change,

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:10.970
they like their own traces more.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:15.450
Even if the traces are
coming from a better model,

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:19.110
they tend to their traces, their
own generated traces, more.

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:21.350
And we're going to
have talk about Swirl.

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.010
This is one of the papers that--

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:25.250
The multi-step reasoning.

00:38:25.250 --> 00:38:27.270
The multi-step reasoning
that we talk about,

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:29.250
what if we bring
a different model

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:32.130
not in the context of
generating reasoning traces,

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:36.430
but in the context of
evaluating and giving feedback?

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:38.410
And we're going to
see that as well.

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:41.850
But models, surprisingly
or not surprisingly,

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:44.710
they like their own
traces a lot more.

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:48.250
For reasoning models, how
are they taught to reason?

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:49.990
Is it a hard coded
set of sequences

00:38:49.990 --> 00:38:52.270
that are taught to do
traces, or are they

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:54.470
somehow fine tuned
to say, we need

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:57.090
to do this much or
this kind of thing,

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:59.058
which will cause the
traces to generate?

00:38:59.058 --> 00:39:01.350
So I don't think there's a
published piece of work that

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:03.670
really covers this bit.

00:39:03.670 --> 00:39:05.070
It's both, yeah.

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:06.370
But it's a bit of both.

00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:08.350
And at the same
time, the base model

00:39:08.350 --> 00:39:12.990
did have the thinking
capability to begin with, right.

00:39:12.990 --> 00:39:14.882
Go do some amount of thinking.

00:39:14.882 --> 00:39:16.590
One thing that we will
cover in the class

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:19.550
is this notion of outcome
rewards model and process reward

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:24.990
model, and how you can use that
feedback to get the model back.

00:39:24.990 --> 00:39:27.350
Yeah, there's always
some bootstrapping.

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:29.790
Like, OK, here are
different ways.

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:32.310
Chain of thought
itself, which is heavily

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:35.830
used in the instruction
tuning of data set,

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:39.470
shows the model some
ways of how to think.

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:42.440
And there could be
some templates showing

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:44.180
for the model, fine tuning.

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:48.620
But the things that the model,
if you just ask it any question

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:50.200
and it comes up
with that, there's

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:51.800
a whole lot of
generalization that.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:54.720
So models have gone
way above like what

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:57.780
the data or instructions that
are used for training them.

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:02.740
Yes?

00:40:02.740 --> 00:40:05.840
In sampling, is there a way
to make the number of samples

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:07.320
from--

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:08.640
Please speak up.

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.360
The number of
samples, can they be

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:13.513
dependent on problem difficulty?

00:40:13.513 --> 00:40:15.680
I don't think there's a
published piece of work that

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:16.460
does that.

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:24.400
So there is follow
up work to that

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:27.940
where we use a reward model,
and then you can use that.

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:30.820
So assuming the reward model
has some notion of complexity.

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:32.600
If you haven't
solved, it can guide

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:36.760
more sampling, or whether
it's repeated or parallel.

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:40.220
But that's definitely
an interesting direction

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:41.460
to explore.

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:43.580
Yeah.

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:47.340
OK, we need to get to
the-- oh, we haven't--

00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:49.820
yeah, let me cover this.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:53.440
OK, so you now know about
large language models.

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:56.520
And then you heard about how
they learned how to think,

00:40:56.520 --> 00:40:58.540
and then they learned
how to reason.

00:40:58.540 --> 00:41:01.720
So what's next, and why
is this course relevant?

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:03.500
So a lot of what
this course is about

00:41:03.500 --> 00:41:07.660
is that large language models,
as chatbots or as reasoning

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:10.740
models, are basically
still single turn or just

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:12.000
in the chat format.

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.160
So they're not accomplishing
a task for you.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:17.020
They're fun to interact with,
but they're not necessarily

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:18.780
accomplishing a task for you.

00:41:18.780 --> 00:41:22.120
What has happened this year
almost in the last few months,

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:24.740
and it's been surprising,
is that agents

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:28.580
like Cloud Code or Deep Research
have really enabled people

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:30.700
to do real world workflows.

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:32.220
So they're agentic
workflows which

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:35.260
can achieve tasks that you
ask them to do end to end.

00:41:35.260 --> 00:41:38.670
So, for example, if you want
to go research and figure out,

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:44.290
where should I rent a home
perhaps for the entire year,

00:41:44.290 --> 00:41:47.510
if I want to take a
class at Stanford,

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:50.550
the model would actually be able
to do a whole bunch of analysis

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:53.230
and go look at a lot
of different websites

00:41:53.230 --> 00:41:54.910
and actually
summarize the results

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:57.350
and give you pros and
cons of different places.

00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:59.290
This used to not
be possible before.

00:41:59.290 --> 00:42:02.710
So this will cover a little bit
of how that becomes possible

00:42:02.710 --> 00:42:05.310
and how this course allows
you to learn about that.

00:42:05.310 --> 00:42:08.150
And then Cloud Code for
example, if any of you

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:11.250
are Codex, which is the
coding agent from OpenAI,

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:13.030
if you're using
that, what you'll see

00:42:13.030 --> 00:42:16.090
is that just by giving
instructions in English,

00:42:16.090 --> 00:42:19.310
you can modify files, or you
can figure out test cases

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:20.130
and whatnot.

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:23.710
So it really has become a
coding productivity tool

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:25.830
in the day-to-day workflows
for software engineers

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:28.070
at this point in time.

00:42:28.070 --> 00:42:31.610
So what is the transition
from LLM to agents?

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:34.270
So as I was giving you the
example of deep research

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:40.450
and of coding agents,
basically now, the model

00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:41.810
can be given a goal.

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:44.850
And it will plan out the
steps that it will go interact

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:46.530
with the environment.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:49.650
And based on the feedback, it
will correct its steps until it

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:51.650
achieves the goals or it
will come back and say,

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:53.090
I cannot achieve the goal.

00:42:53.090 --> 00:42:57.290
So this notion of having
some notion of a goal,

00:42:57.290 --> 00:43:00.730
taking actions towards that
goal, getting the feedback,

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:03.090
and then deciding
when to stop, that's

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:07.330
what makes agents different
from the chatbot types of what

00:43:07.330 --> 00:43:08.690
we were doing before.

00:43:08.690 --> 00:43:12.610
And this also might
require interacting

00:43:12.610 --> 00:43:17.070
with tools which are external to
what the model itself is doing,

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:18.630
getting some inputs from there.

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:21.570
But it still stays on
track of whatever task

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:23.410
it's choosing to
accomplish, which

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:26.450
might mean that it has to
have some form of memory

00:43:26.450 --> 00:43:30.370
to keep track of the tasks
that it's trying to accomplish.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:32.550
So what has been accomplished?

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:35.360
So in several cases,
in simpler cases,

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:39.060
you can achieve these
end to end goals.

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:41.760
Deep research can be
accomplished end to end.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.580
But in most scenarios,
you are still having

00:43:43.580 --> 00:43:44.840
very static workflows.

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:48.220
So today, what exists is closer
to agentic workflows, where

00:43:48.220 --> 00:43:50.500
you have some sort
of an input where

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:51.920
you're giving a model the goal.

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:54.860
And then what this
slide is showing you

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:57.700
is that one model
is perhaps giving

00:43:57.700 --> 00:43:59.860
the output like a solution,
and then another model

00:43:59.860 --> 00:44:01.900
is judging it and
then deciding based

00:44:01.900 --> 00:44:04.120
on that, whether the
solution should be accepted.

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:07.900
So that's one orchestration
framework that's possible.

00:44:07.900 --> 00:44:10.100
Deep research would be more
of the second one, where

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:12.500
you basically generating--

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:16.340
you're calling the LLM on
multiple possible inputs

00:44:16.340 --> 00:44:19.447
and then aggregating
the things to get

00:44:19.447 --> 00:44:21.780
the output, which will be a
summary of the deep research

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:22.280
results.

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:25.300
So this is a very
cartoonish abstraction

00:44:25.300 --> 00:44:27.303
of what agentic workflows
might look like.

00:44:27.303 --> 00:44:28.720
And you can
generalize from there.

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:32.400
But instead of having
very open ended loop

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:34.157
that I was showing
you here, where you're

00:44:34.157 --> 00:44:36.240
supposed to go take an
action from the environment

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:39.280
and come back and
do some feedback,

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:41.520
it's easier for
open ended problems

00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:44.240
to construct this graph by
hand of how a human would

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:47.920
do it and then get this feedback
perhaps from an LLM, which

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:50.020
is the LLM evaluator here.

00:44:50.020 --> 00:44:52.320
So a lot of the
real world workflows

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:54.060
still have this paradigm.

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:57.440
But in certain cases, we are
starting to see signs of life

00:44:57.440 --> 00:44:59.780
for what's possible
in this slide.

00:44:59.780 --> 00:45:03.240
And coding being one and
research being another one.

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:07.420
So typically, the workflows
will have things like LLM calls.

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:08.880
So you would
basically call an LLM

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:11.300
with an instruction
or some form of input.

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:14.100
And then you're asking the
LLM to give you an output.

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:15.780
It will have some
form of verifiers.

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:18.600
We'll cover verifiers in
a whole lecture by itself.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:21.160
It will have some form of
critics or judges, which

00:45:21.160 --> 00:45:24.560
is effectively LLM
as a judge paradigm.

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:25.780
There might be tool calls.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:27.280
For example, for
deep research, you

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:29.160
have to actually
go search the web

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:32.020
to figure out what
exactly is the content

00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:33.270
that you should be looking at.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:34.978
So there might be tool
calls of that form

00:45:34.978 --> 00:45:41.610
or getting some value around
what exactly is the weather.

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:44.210
And similarly, search
would be another tool call.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.090
And then you would
orchestrate these

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:47.710
in some form of a workflow.

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:50.910
So a simplest workflow here
would be prompt chaining.

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:54.050
So similar to reasoning models
where the task gets decomposed

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:56.250
into subtasks, prompt
chaining would simply

00:45:56.250 --> 00:45:58.650
be like you have chained a
bunch of different subtasks

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:00.450
that you're supposed
to go accomplish

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:02.370
to achieve the end to end goal.

00:46:02.370 --> 00:46:03.430
There might be routing.

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:06.710
Routing works for complex
tasks where you say, OK,

00:46:06.710 --> 00:46:10.090
if this is very complex, then
go do this more complicated set

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:11.010
of LLM calls.

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:15.250
And it's less complicated
than do this simpler workflow.

00:46:15.250 --> 00:46:18.090
There might be parallelization
where deep research is

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:21.170
one example where you can
have multiple LLM calls work

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:24.290
simultaneously on researching
different keywords

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:26.390
that you give as
input to the LLM.

00:46:26.390 --> 00:46:29.130
And then finally, you
aggregate the output.

00:46:29.130 --> 00:46:31.990
Or you might break the task
into independent subtasks

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:34.910
and then combine the solution.

00:46:34.910 --> 00:46:37.190
Or you might have this
notion of orchestrator,

00:46:37.190 --> 00:46:39.590
where you basically
are actually using

00:46:39.590 --> 00:46:43.890
some sort of a, in simple
words, LLM manager.

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:46.090
So a central LLM is
actually doing the planning.

00:46:46.090 --> 00:46:48.230
So in Cloud Code, you
actually start to see that,

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.930
that there is some notion of
a plan that it comes up with.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:55.510
And then based on that plan, it
will make subsequent LLM calls.

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:57.530
You might have an
evaluator or a judge.

00:46:57.530 --> 00:46:59.910
So instead of getting
feedback from real world,

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.730
like user or from some sort of
a actual running a unit test,

00:47:03.730 --> 00:47:05.930
it actually might just
use LLM as a judge.

00:47:05.930 --> 00:47:09.490
And we will have some homeworks
that will cover this aspect.

00:47:09.490 --> 00:47:11.050
And then you might
have verifiers.

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:14.530
Verifiers are things where you
can actually verify the output.

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:17.540
So in code, for example,
if you run the code,

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:19.290
how do you know that
this code is correct?

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:22.070
So typically, as software
developers we write unit tests.

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:26.080
So similarly, verifiers might be
running some kind of unit test

00:47:26.080 --> 00:47:29.320
to check, whether what
LLM generated is correct.

00:47:29.320 --> 00:47:33.440
And in domains which are
verifiable, math, code,

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:35.720
and other domains that
are more rule based,

00:47:35.720 --> 00:47:38.080
this verification is a
good way to give feedback

00:47:38.080 --> 00:47:42.400
back to the model so that
it can correct its steps.

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:47.360
So most of these workflows
mean that the LLM

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:49.080
needs to be better at planning.

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:51.140
It needs to be better
at multi-step reasoning,

00:47:51.140 --> 00:47:52.900
and it needs to be better
at self-improvement.

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:54.240
Like when it makes
mistakes, it needs

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:55.620
to be able to correct itself.

00:47:55.620 --> 00:47:59.200
So effectively, these
are new paradigms

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:02.160
that the current set of
LLMs just with reasoning

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:04.180
were not quite accomplishing.

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:06.120
And these are some of
the topics we do plan

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:09.800
to cover in subsequent lectures.

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:13.700
And just to drive the point
home around coding agents.

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:17.000
So this is a very
simple example.

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:19.100
I should have updated
the slide for Cloud Code.

00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:20.800
But basically what
this is showing

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:23.820
is that you have an
LLM agent and it's

00:48:23.820 --> 00:48:25.743
interacting with the computer.

00:48:25.743 --> 00:48:27.160
These days just
with the terminal,

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:29.618
you give it an instruction
where you might tell it that you

00:48:29.618 --> 00:48:31.420
want to implement a test.

00:48:31.420 --> 00:48:35.000
And then it has some
navigation of repositories,

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:36.580
searching of files,
all of these tool

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:39.080
calls around viewing
files, editing lines.

00:48:39.080 --> 00:48:40.580
And then it will
go execute a bunch

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:42.100
of commands in the terminal.

00:48:42.100 --> 00:48:44.120
And based on the output,
it might be like,

00:48:44.120 --> 00:48:46.880
OK, I need to go
edit this other file,

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:48.720
or I need to go look
at this other file.

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:50.820
So if you look at this
loop, this very much

00:48:50.820 --> 00:48:55.263
mirrors what was-- so this was
not quite reliable last year.

00:48:55.263 --> 00:48:57.180
And it's just starting
to get reliable in what

00:48:57.180 --> 00:49:00.980
we are seeing in the realm
of coding agents now.

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:01.660
Yes?

00:49:01.660 --> 00:49:05.660
Can I ask why do you
think it's not reliable?

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:08.580
The general architecture
is, I would say,

00:49:08.580 --> 00:49:11.460
not changed that much.

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:14.240
I mean, I think the paradigm
is very much the same.

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:17.340
It's mostly a matter of more
powerful models and then

00:49:17.340 --> 00:49:20.180
better RL.

00:49:20.180 --> 00:49:22.510
RL with verifiable
rewards is working.

00:49:22.510 --> 00:49:25.123
The train time scaling
is working well.

00:49:25.123 --> 00:49:26.110
I see.

00:49:26.110 --> 00:49:28.510
How did you [INAUDIBLE]?

00:49:37.830 --> 00:49:40.190
I mean, computer data, right.

00:49:40.190 --> 00:49:41.050
One of those two.

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:44.330
But just also, once the
models start to get better,

00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:47.590
there's this self-improvement
loop that kicks in.

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:50.030
Because you can now generate
tests, and the tests

00:49:50.030 --> 00:49:51.090
become more reliable.

00:49:51.090 --> 00:49:52.570
So in the published
pieces of work,

00:49:52.570 --> 00:49:53.987
if you look at,
say, code monkeys,

00:49:53.987 --> 00:49:56.510
which we covered
last year, if you

00:49:56.510 --> 00:49:59.390
can generate unit
tests for whatever code

00:49:59.390 --> 00:50:05.430
the model generated and then see
if the generated unit tests are

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:08.030
making things better, then
that's a very good way

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:09.750
to verify things, right?

00:50:09.750 --> 00:50:11.250
Yeah, model based.

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:16.310
It seems like to some extent,
if we have good verifiers,

00:50:16.310 --> 00:50:21.810
we can make the model
generate the right code.

00:50:21.810 --> 00:50:23.550
But the question is,
how do we do that?

00:50:23.550 --> 00:50:25.110
And there are ways to do it.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:29.410
And we would be
happy for you guys

00:50:29.410 --> 00:50:31.790
to take research projects
in that domain, too.

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:34.790
But obviously, it's an
active research area.

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:38.050
But there are ways to
make improvements that--

00:50:38.050 --> 00:50:40.650
I think what you're alluding
to is this notion of generator

00:50:40.650 --> 00:50:41.630
verifier gaps.

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:43.330
So it's easy for
models to generate

00:50:43.330 --> 00:50:47.290
a whole bunch of nonsense or
sensible set of reasoning traces

00:50:47.290 --> 00:50:49.610
or useful set of content.

00:50:49.610 --> 00:50:51.950
But at the end of the day,
whether that's useful or not,

00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:53.390
we need feedback loop for that.

00:50:53.390 --> 00:50:56.230
And if you're creative writing,
how much feedback can you get?

00:50:56.230 --> 00:50:58.570
So human feedback ends
up becoming a bottleneck.

00:50:58.570 --> 00:51:01.070
In domains where you
can have good feedback,

00:51:01.070 --> 00:51:03.570
that's where it's possible to
continue to improve the model.

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:06.930
And that but we're getting
robust verification,

00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:08.950
a lot of robust
verification is hard.

00:51:08.950 --> 00:51:12.050
So Azalia will cover a paper
that she did in her lab

00:51:12.050 --> 00:51:14.410
about how to combine verifiers.

00:51:14.410 --> 00:51:16.450
But verification
continues to be one

00:51:16.450 --> 00:51:20.500
of the bottlenecks in this
space to make them better.

00:51:20.500 --> 00:51:22.580
Yes?

00:51:22.580 --> 00:51:24.980
Intuitively, I
think free training,

00:51:24.980 --> 00:51:29.260
you get ability to survive
at such a large corpus.

00:51:29.260 --> 00:51:31.580
So just to me, intuitively,
I'm trying to figure out

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:33.860
why there's such a big jump.

00:51:33.860 --> 00:51:36.940
Because we're providing
a reward signal,

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:38.620
and we're trying to
elicit activities

00:51:38.620 --> 00:51:43.660
that shouldn't
exist in training.

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:45.760
So I guess I'm just not--

00:51:48.740 --> 00:51:51.320
I think this is still an
active area of research.

00:51:51.320 --> 00:51:54.340
So there are different set
of opinions around what

00:51:54.340 --> 00:51:55.440
really improves the model.

00:51:55.440 --> 00:51:57.980
Is it RL, or is it
the pre-training,

00:51:57.980 --> 00:52:00.020
the diverse data by itself?

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:02.900
And I don't think there is
a single point of consensus.

00:52:02.900 --> 00:52:06.820
At this point in time,
both processes help.

00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:09.360
What you just said, which I
will repeat for the class,

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:12.220
is that if pre-training
is the place where

00:52:12.220 --> 00:52:13.800
the model, after
repeated sampling,

00:52:13.800 --> 00:52:16.410
should be able to at least have
one solution correct out of

00:52:16.410 --> 00:52:18.410
if it was generating a
large number of samples,

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:21.030
then this notion
of giving feedback

00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:22.995
should improve the
passive one accuracy,

00:52:22.995 --> 00:52:24.370
but should not
improve the model.

00:52:24.370 --> 00:52:26.110
Why is it such a big jump?

00:52:26.110 --> 00:52:30.095
But I think that whole loop is
not completely well understood.

00:52:30.095 --> 00:52:31.470
It's like the
first signs of life

00:52:31.470 --> 00:52:33.170
and it starts to
get commercialized.

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:35.590
But I think there's a
lot more research still

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:36.558
open in this area.

00:52:36.558 --> 00:52:37.350
Yeah, that's right.

00:52:37.350 --> 00:52:39.970
So that's only one way
of thinking about it.

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:42.870
There are at least
some signs of life

00:52:42.870 --> 00:52:45.630
which say that you
can continue to do RL,

00:52:45.630 --> 00:52:47.570
and that will continue
to improve the model.

00:52:47.570 --> 00:52:48.070
Yeah.

00:52:50.590 --> 00:52:51.170
OK.

00:52:51.170 --> 00:52:53.630
Yeah.

00:52:53.630 --> 00:52:57.090
So this is basically covering a
lot of the same set of points.

00:52:57.090 --> 00:52:59.430
But one abstraction
that is worth

00:52:59.430 --> 00:53:01.550
taking away from
this particular chart

00:53:01.550 --> 00:53:04.150
is that even when you
give the model a goal,

00:53:04.150 --> 00:53:06.090
it has to clarify
the user intent.

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.610
So it's not always obvious to
it what the model user wants.

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:12.030
And then it might go
search for relevant files.

00:53:12.030 --> 00:53:16.610
And then whatever a set of
actions which chooses to take,

00:53:16.610 --> 00:53:18.670
oftentimes, it needs some
form of verification.

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:22.350
In this particular case, it's
based on passing the test.

00:53:22.350 --> 00:53:25.510
And it might actually generate
the test that it needs to pass,

00:53:25.510 --> 00:53:29.710
which is also mentioned here.

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:32.948
But the key idea is that
if you give it a task,

00:53:32.948 --> 00:53:34.490
how does it come
back, and how do you

00:53:34.490 --> 00:53:37.250
know that it's going to
complete what you asked it for?

00:53:37.250 --> 00:53:40.690
Oftentimes, the users will not
specify the problem well enough.

00:53:40.690 --> 00:53:43.330
So clarifying the
user intent so that it

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:45.970
knows what to go look
for and how to verify

00:53:45.970 --> 00:53:47.950
starts to become important.

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:49.570
And in models like
O3, I think they

00:53:49.570 --> 00:53:51.550
have seen a lot of
these traces end to end.

00:53:51.550 --> 00:53:53.850
So they are able to do the
planning, the reasoning,

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:56.350
the multi-step reasoning, and
come back with the conclusion.

00:53:56.350 --> 00:54:01.650
But a lot of this is needed
for completing tasks end to end

00:54:01.650 --> 00:54:03.890
or having an end to end goal.

00:54:03.890 --> 00:54:05.570
And where this has
been super useful

00:54:05.570 --> 00:54:10.570
is if you have repetitive tasks
like code migrations or version

00:54:10.570 --> 00:54:13.500
upgrades, or if you need to
restructure the code base.

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:16.480
Or if you have tasks that
involve data engineering,

00:54:16.480 --> 00:54:19.900
where you have to basically do
some sort of extract the data

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:22.360
and then do some sort
of cleanup on the data.

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:25.520
Or if you have to do some sort
of data warehouse migration.

00:54:25.520 --> 00:54:29.480
So a lot of this work tends
to be extremely repetitive.

00:54:29.480 --> 00:54:33.260
And it's much easier to just
delegate it to coding agents.

00:54:33.260 --> 00:54:34.480
Same for unit tasks.

00:54:34.480 --> 00:54:39.020
Oftentimes much easier to start
to give it to coding agents.

00:54:39.020 --> 00:54:42.740
Another area where agents have
become extremely prevalent

00:54:42.740 --> 00:54:44.440
is in customer support.

00:54:44.440 --> 00:54:46.140
It's one of the
most thankless jobs

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:49.180
that when you are on the
customer support side of things

00:54:49.180 --> 00:54:53.540
have to do, and using LLMs to
do that definitely streamlines

00:54:53.540 --> 00:54:55.120
the experience in
interesting ways.

00:54:55.120 --> 00:54:57.380
So one simple set
of examples ends up

00:54:57.380 --> 00:54:59.600
being like you can use them
to do live transcription.

00:54:59.600 --> 00:55:01.280
So that gives you
a very nice record.

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:04.580
And you're seeing that even
for meetings these days.

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:08.080
Another simple example is that
you can have Knowledge Assist.

00:55:08.080 --> 00:55:10.300
So if you have a
database of information,

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:13.500
the customer support agent does
not need to know everything.

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:16.760
They can consult with
the LLM and get an answer

00:55:16.760 --> 00:55:19.560
and surface the
relevant article, which

00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:24.160
is better than having just
search, an index and search

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.640
by itself.

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:27.580
Smart reply.

00:55:27.580 --> 00:55:30.820
So in chat, oftentimes, this
has existed for a while,

00:55:30.820 --> 00:55:34.320
but you can use an agent
to give chat responses.

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:38.200
And then having a call
summary can help you--

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:41.520
you can use the call summary
to really improve the customer

00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:43.060
experience by itself as well.

00:55:43.060 --> 00:55:46.260
So overall, I think in
the customer support area,

00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:49.420
there's multiple companies
that are going after this area.

00:55:49.420 --> 00:55:52.080
But there are different
segments of the problem that

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:54.890
can be addressed using LLMs,
and they have been super useful.

00:55:54.890 --> 00:55:56.640
And then there's also
the end to end stuff

00:55:56.640 --> 00:55:59.480
that's starting to happen here.

00:55:59.480 --> 00:56:01.743
And then the third
example that we will use

00:56:01.743 --> 00:56:03.160
and actually some
of the homeworks

00:56:03.160 --> 00:56:05.320
is that if you have
very complex topics

00:56:05.320 --> 00:56:08.647
and you want to provide
a comprehensive report,

00:56:08.647 --> 00:56:11.230
earlier, it used to be that you
had to do a literature review,

00:56:11.230 --> 00:56:12.790
and then you had to
summarize each paper,

00:56:12.790 --> 00:56:14.390
and then you had to
synthesize these things.

00:56:14.390 --> 00:56:16.490
And these days, you can
just give it to an LLM

00:56:16.490 --> 00:56:17.690
and actually do it for you.

00:56:17.690 --> 00:56:21.290
In the study, maybe more
articles than you would.

00:56:21.290 --> 00:56:25.190
So typically, if you give
it an example like, say,

00:56:25.190 --> 00:56:27.430
2022 Winter Olympics
opening ceremony,

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:30.550
it will identify what
references to go look at.

00:56:30.550 --> 00:56:33.370
Then it will construct an
outline of, OK, these references

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:34.970
are relevant or not.

00:56:34.970 --> 00:56:37.730
And then summarize
each of the references

00:56:37.730 --> 00:56:39.710
as to what is the
relevant content,

00:56:39.710 --> 00:56:42.970
and then combine them to
create a full length article.

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:45.990
Which is very impressive
in certain cases,

00:56:45.990 --> 00:56:49.810
and you'll get to try it
in one of the homeworks.

00:56:49.810 --> 00:56:53.370
And then even more
forward looking.

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:55.250
These research
agents are starting

00:56:55.250 --> 00:56:58.470
to be used as AI scientists.

00:56:58.470 --> 00:57:02.210
So basically, they're starting
to assist scientists in, say,

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:04.910
solving math problems or
solving science problems.

00:57:04.910 --> 00:57:08.150
So here, the LLM is used
as a brainstorming thing

00:57:08.150 --> 00:57:09.250
to come up with ideas.

00:57:09.250 --> 00:57:12.150
So the idea generation phase,
then the experiment iteration

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:15.470
phase, it might actually help
you iterate on the experiments

00:57:15.470 --> 00:57:17.410
that you are hoping to work on.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:19.370
This is from the
AI scientist paper.

00:57:19.370 --> 00:57:21.290
And then in the
paper writeup phase,

00:57:21.290 --> 00:57:27.990
it will help you improve
the paper writer by itself.

00:57:27.990 --> 00:57:32.990
And what's interesting is
that even though these models

00:57:32.990 --> 00:57:36.030
hallucinate just the notion
that they can come up

00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:39.570
with so many different set
of ideas, as an AI scientist,

00:57:39.570 --> 00:57:41.910
it might actually
brainstorm or give you

00:57:41.910 --> 00:57:45.190
ideas outside what you would
have thought if you had just

00:57:45.190 --> 00:57:47.590
taken a bunch of courses
or if you're a researcher

00:57:47.590 --> 00:57:49.670
and you've been in the
field for a long time.

00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:53.107
Sometimes reading the
web allows these alarms

00:57:53.107 --> 00:57:55.190
to come up with ideas that
are way outside the box

00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:58.870
and can be a very good
brainstorming way.

00:57:58.870 --> 00:58:02.150
So these have been super
useful in the AI scientist

00:58:02.150 --> 00:58:04.000
style of work.

00:58:04.000 --> 00:58:07.340
OK, so let's move
to course logistics.

00:58:07.340 --> 00:58:09.960
But before that, do
we have any questions?

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:11.500
Yes?

00:58:11.500 --> 00:58:12.660
I just want to clarify.

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:15.940
So is reasoning and chain of
thought linked in training,

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:18.200
or is it just a
prompt engineering

00:58:18.200 --> 00:58:19.880
that was accidentally
discovering

00:58:19.880 --> 00:58:22.200
these kind of capabilities
in larger models?

00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:25.920
And if so, are there other
emergent behaviors, sites,

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:30.760
or reasoning that you
think might be discovered?

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:35.880
So I mean, it was not
baked in by design.

00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:39.600
It was basically discovered.

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:40.980
I mean, we gave
it hard problems,

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:43.020
and then we saw that
it was basically--

00:58:43.020 --> 00:58:45.020
by having reasoning chains,
it was doing better.

00:58:45.020 --> 00:58:49.240
So the GSM 8-K was the first
paper that showed signs of life

00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:49.740
of this.

00:58:49.740 --> 00:58:52.620
And then with larger
model like PaLM,

00:58:52.620 --> 00:58:55.180
we actually saw that
this was a very big deal.

00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:58.120
In one of the examples there
was that it could explain jokes,

00:58:58.120 --> 00:58:59.520
which was very impressive.

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:03.420
And then from there, the
reasoning models have emerged.

00:59:03.420 --> 00:59:06.780
But it has read all of the
web, so it has definitely

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:11.060
seen data, which is more
methodical and systematic.

00:59:11.060 --> 00:59:13.940
But the reasoning
models are trained to be

00:59:13.940 --> 00:59:18.100
reasoning more and more, right.

00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:21.160
The entire reasoning is
not an emergent behavior.

00:59:21.160 --> 00:59:25.100
The reasoning models, they're
trained to be thinking.

00:59:25.100 --> 00:59:27.040
But the models are converging.

00:59:27.040 --> 00:59:29.900
So the models are going to be
trained such that they know when

00:59:29.900 --> 00:59:32.680
they need a lot of reasoning
and when they don't.

00:59:32.680 --> 00:59:35.640
And to generate the answers.

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:39.480
But chain of thought originally
was an emergent behavior.

00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:42.700
They noticed that, oh,
if we explain things,

00:59:42.700 --> 00:59:45.340
a model gets better.

00:59:45.340 --> 00:59:49.000
Are there any emergent
behaviors in larger models?

00:59:49.000 --> 00:59:52.200
I don't think I would see it
as emergent behaviors per se.

00:59:52.200 --> 00:59:54.418
But I think as I was
mentioning, we usually

00:59:54.418 --> 00:59:55.960
go looking for
certain things, right.

00:59:55.960 --> 00:59:58.300
So in the agentic workflows,
what we're looking for

00:59:58.300 --> 01:00:00.930
is planning, which is
a form of reasoning.

01:00:00.930 --> 01:00:02.870
We're looking for
multi-step reasoning, which

01:00:02.870 --> 01:00:04.198
we will cover in class as well.

01:00:04.198 --> 01:00:05.990
And then we're looking
for self-improvement

01:00:05.990 --> 01:00:07.190
or self-correction.

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:09.690
So all of these capabilities
would be nice to have.

01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:12.910
And what gets the models
there is a set of questions

01:00:12.910 --> 01:00:13.910
that are worth--

01:00:13.910 --> 01:00:16.110
there are papers on
this kind of thing.

01:00:16.110 --> 01:00:19.970
Like self-correction,
backtracking, things like that.

01:00:19.970 --> 01:00:23.250
It's like yes, you
could call it emergent,

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:25.810
but you could also
say, think about it,

01:00:25.810 --> 01:00:28.150
that they have seen
this kind of behavior.

01:00:28.150 --> 01:00:32.290
And it's like it's reinforced in
the way that they're fine tuned.

01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:38.230
So yeah, it's hard to say.

